WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
A weather office logged one integer temperature reading per station. Two stations make a close call when their

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.713
readings differ by at most tolerance. Count the pairs of positions i &lt; j that make a close

3
00:00:10.713 --> 00:00:16.984
call. Stations with equal readings are still separate stations, so equal readings form a pair too.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.500
The close calls are the two 3s, 9 with 11, 11 with 12, and 12 with 14. Pairs

5
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.537
such as 9 and 12 differ by 3 and do not count, so the answer is 4.

6
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.944
All three readings are equal, so each of the 3 position pairs is a close call even with

7
00:00:35.944 --> 00:00:36.882
tolerance 0.

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.385
In sorted order, the readings within tolerance below a given reading are one contiguous block ending right

9
00:00:44.385 --> 00:00:44.830
before it.

10
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.418
Sort a copy of the readings. For each right index, advance left while sorted[right] - sorted[left] exceeds

11
00:00:52.418 --> 00:00:57.772
tolerance. Every index from left to right - 1 pairs with right, so add right - left.

12
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.952
Sorting integer readings with sorted() makes the eligible partners for each right endpoint a contiguous range.

13
00:01:04.952 --> 00:01:11.260
Its left boundary only moves forward, so the sweep is linear after the O(n log n) sort. Python

14
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:17.327
sorting uses numeric order for integers. Since the readings have a small bounded range, a counting array with

15
00:01:17.327 --> 00:01:22.450
prefix sums is another option; this sorted sweep also works without relying on that range.

16
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.697
The example becomes [3, 3, 9, 11, 12, 14, 20].

17
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:38.657
The second 3 adds 1, 9 adds 0, 11 adds 1, 12 adds 1 (11 only), 14 adds

18
00:01:38.657 --> 00:01:42.071
1 (12 only) and 20 adds 0, giving 4.

19
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.765
O(n log n) for the sort plus O(n) for the sweep. O(n) for the sorted copy
